00:34:12.740 --> 00:34:15.100
it tells you what it is in the most general sense.

00:34:15.560 --> 00:34:23.320
And then the, the art is clear enough that you don't really need that explanation of like

00:34:23.900 --> 00:34:28.920
uh, like instead of looking at the radio and say like, this is a radio, it looks like it's

00:34:28.920 --> 00:34:29.780
20 years old.

00:34:30.040 --> 00:34:32.540
It kind of, it looks like I could probably break it open.

00:34:32.700 --> 00:34:37.440
Like, no, it's just, you see a radio, you can press open radio if you want and you'll

00:34:37.440 --> 00:34:39.060
get batteries or something out of it.

00:34:39.380 --> 00:34:43.020
But, uh, yeah, I don't think it really needed that inspect button.

00:34:43.720 --> 00:34:47.900
Yeah. The only reason that I ever wanted it was like, sometimes there would be like, there would

00:34:47.900 --> 00:34:50.920
be, I mean, there'd be like a poster and I feel like, I feel like most of the time

00:34:50.920 --> 00:34:55.260
read worked on that, but then other times, other times it didn't, or like, I, like there

00:34:55.260 --> 00:34:56.320
were just times where I wasn't sure.

00:34:56.440 --> 00:34:59.520
Like, is this something I, I don't know, like, what is this?

00:34:59.640 --> 00:35:00.520
You know what I mean?

00:35:00.560 --> 00:35:04.300
Like sometimes I'd be like, you know, there'd be something on the wall and I would be like,

00:35:04.360 --> 00:35:05.240
do I read this?

00:35:05.320 --> 00:35:07.040
Is this a picture? Is this a switch?

00:35:07.180 --> 00:35:08.440
Like, I can't tell what this is.

00:35:08.560 --> 00:35:12.260
Cause, cause some of it is like hard to interpret.

00:35:12.400 --> 00:35:17.780
Like, I don't think I, it hindered my ability to do anything, but I, what I, I did several

00:35:17.780 --> 00:35:20.400
times be like, can I just look at this and tell me what this is?

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:23.840
Because whenever you hover over it, it at least gives you a name of the thing.

00:35:24.700 --> 00:35:26.180
Yeah. That's true.

00:35:26.260 --> 00:35:30.020
But like, there's like picture, like there's pictures of, at the top of the stairs and I

00:35:30.020 --> 00:35:31.580
don't think they actually ended up mattering.

00:35:31.700 --> 00:35:34.880
So like, maybe this is me wanting something that wouldn't have helped because it was never

00:35:34.880 --> 00:35:39.220
actually needed. But at the time I wasn't sure if it was needed, but there's pictures at the top of the first

00:35:39.220 --> 00:35:43.000
set of stairs of, of Edna and I don't know, son, whatever.

00:35:43.520 --> 00:35:44.600
I don't know. Yeah.

00:35:44.760 --> 00:35:45.760
Whatever. Whoever these people are.

00:35:45.760 --> 00:35:51.640
And like, I'm, I want to see like what there is, like what it is, but it's pixelated.

00:35:51.780 --> 00:35:52.640
So it's hard to really tell.

00:35:52.780 --> 00:35:56.840
I don't know. There were just a few times where I'm like, yeah, I'm just like, I would like to be able

00:35:56.840 --> 00:35:57.780
to, there's a wanted poster.

00:35:57.800 --> 00:35:58.740
I mean, that's obviously Reed.

00:35:59.180 --> 00:36:01.720
There were definitely situations and I cannot think of one right now.

00:36:01.720 --> 00:36:05.940
There are definitely situations where I was like, I want to like look at this thing to

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:06.940
just to see what it is.

00:36:07.520 --> 00:36:09.540
I can't believe we couldn't smell anything.

00:36:09.820 --> 00:36:12.660
I don't think that that's a joke.

00:36:13.040 --> 00:36:14.600
Sniff. I don't think that's appropriate.

00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:16.260
Should have been able to sniff everything knowing that.

00:36:16.720 --> 00:36:17.640
And then you'd have it described.

00:36:17.960 --> 00:36:19.460
Well, I could see some hilarious situations.

00:36:19.460 --> 00:36:21.200
You microwave the hamster and then you sniff.

00:36:21.820 --> 00:36:23.400
Yeah. That's, that's inappropriate.

00:36:24.360 --> 00:36:27.800
Speaking of inappropriate and censorships, it goes beyond the hamster.

00:36:27.880 --> 00:36:31.120
Obviously the hamster is just obvious, a very famous example.

00:36:31.840 --> 00:36:36.100
There were like Playboy calendars.

00:36:36.340 --> 00:36:38.360
There was, or maybe it wasn't Playboy.

00:36:38.500 --> 00:36:39.400
Maybe it was like a swimsuit.

00:36:39.900 --> 00:36:41.240
No, it was a swimsuit calendar.

00:36:41.240 --> 00:36:43.660
It was turned into a regular calendar in this game.

00:36:44.280 --> 00:36:48.040
There was a sculpture with boobies, like all that stuff out.

00:36:48.320 --> 00:36:50.080
Can't have it in my Nintendo game.

00:36:50.860 --> 00:36:55.620
Which is funny in a way that Nintendo had to be like, like, I don't know.

00:36:55.800 --> 00:36:57.140
I feel like occasionally we've seen.

00:36:58.500 --> 00:36:59.900
We have. Yeah.

00:37:00.100 --> 00:37:01.940
Not full frontal, but we've seen enough.

00:37:03.660 --> 00:37:05.860
Yeah. Weren't there like mermaids in some game or something?

00:37:06.140 --> 00:37:12.700
Yeah. Right. Yeah. Do you guys know about the television adaptation of Maniac Mansion?

00:37:13.280 --> 00:37:14.180
I do.

00:37:15.060 --> 00:37:17.920
Did you watch the 10th anniversary special?

00:37:18.260 --> 00:37:21.360
Very confused if that was actually the first episode or not.

00:37:22.260 --> 00:37:23.860
I didn't watch any of it.

00:37:23.960 --> 00:37:28.680
I just had it read to me on a Wikipedia article of like what this was.

00:37:29.160 --> 00:37:32.500
Yeah. I saw some screenshots of it or just like some stills.

00:37:32.500 --> 00:37:36.740
And I wanted to watch it.

00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:40.580
I just, I really, I couldn't fit into my very busy schedule.

00:37:40.880 --> 00:37:42.020
Yes, of course. Tell us.

00:37:42.540 --> 00:37:46.840
Well, what's interesting about it is that the first episode is called the 10th anniversary special.

00:37:47.240 --> 00:37:50.820
And it's meta in all the ways that you can imagine.

00:37:51.660 --> 00:37:55.080
But like, it's also just like, it was confusing to me at first.

00:37:55.200 --> 00:38:00.000
Like, I was like, is this the 10th anniversary special of the first episode of Maniac Mansion?

00:38:00.040 --> 00:38:01.740
Is this actually the first episode?

00:38:01.740 --> 00:38:03.680
Like, that took me a while to like confirm.

00:38:03.920 --> 00:38:06.640
But I did confirm that the episode is just called the 10th anniversary special.

00:38:07.140 --> 00:38:12.560
And what's interesting is that if you were to make a Maniac Mansion TV show, in my opinion,

00:38:12.860 --> 00:38:17.880
you would focus on the kids that went around the mansion and explored it.

00:38:18.040 --> 00:38:21.460
And like, I'm not saying make a whole show where they're walking around a mansion.

00:38:21.480 --> 00:38:22.320
That's boring.

00:38:22.540 --> 00:38:24.400
But like, I would focus on the kids.

00:38:24.680 --> 00:38:25.900
They didn't go that route.

00:38:26.200 --> 00:38:29.260
They didn't focus on Dave and Sandy and all that.

00:38:29.260 --> 00:38:40.300
And what you get is kind of like just the mansion and like the aesthetic of that and a very, very big departure from the game.

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:46.600
Otherwise, to the point where like, obviously, if it's going to be three seasons, like you're going to have to get away from the game.

00:38:46.600 --> 00:38:51.080
But like, man, how did Maniac Mansion, the TV show, get three seasons?

00:38:51.420 --> 00:38:52.620
That is insane.

00:38:52.780 --> 00:38:56.660
And then hearing that like Eugene Levy had a hand in it.

00:38:56.960 --> 00:39:07.280
Yes. Also, like, because when I first saw this coming up and read the Wikipedia article, I saw that like, oh, and there's a TV show.

00:39:08.200 --> 00:39:12.740
And I would never have guessed that the game came first.

00:39:12.740 --> 00:39:22.880
Like, we've played so many TV and movie adaptations that I was like, oh, this must be a really fun way to adapt a TV show with all the characters from the show.

00:39:22.880 --> 00:39:25.180
But it turns out it was the complete opposite.

00:39:25.460 --> 00:39:27.320
Like, I did not know that that would be the case.

00:39:28.580 --> 00:39:29.860
Yeah, I thought the same thing.

00:39:30.300 --> 00:39:33.720
And the show is, you know, it's referential.

00:39:34.080 --> 00:39:35.120
It's a little meta.

00:39:35.460 --> 00:39:38.240
You have characters specifically in the first episode.

00:39:38.320 --> 00:39:41.240
But I believe the trend continues breaking the fourth wall.

00:39:41.940 --> 00:39:48.540
It kind of feels like it's following in like in the footsteps of Twin Peaks a little bit.

00:39:48.800 --> 00:39:49.760
That's what I was going to say.

00:39:50.120 --> 00:39:51.760
It has that kind of vibe to it.

00:39:52.400 --> 00:39:55.020
You know, maybe more, maybe a little more family friendly.

00:39:55.680 --> 00:39:57.400
Although I don't know. But Twin Peaks wasn't out yet.

00:39:58.120 --> 00:39:59.980
Well, when was Twin Peaks?

00:40:00.080 --> 00:40:01.040
Was that 90 as well?

00:40:01.400 --> 00:40:03.080
Yeah, it is. Okay, yeah.

00:40:03.140 --> 00:40:04.420
So they're like fighting each other.

00:40:04.540 --> 00:40:11.420
So technically, I agree with you, Sean, that they couldn't both be like doing it at the same time and copying each other.

00:40:11.420 --> 00:40:12.460
But like, I don't know.

00:40:12.580 --> 00:40:14.520
Maybe that was just a sign of the times, right?

00:40:14.620 --> 00:40:18.660
That like two shows came out very close together and feel kind of related.

00:40:19.580 --> 00:40:21.780
And, you know, loosely related, obviously.

00:40:21.920 --> 00:40:25.740
Well, also, I mean, we're talking about a game that came out on the NES in 1990.

00:40:26.120 --> 00:40:27.420
This was 87.

00:40:27.420 --> 00:40:29.540
87. Yeah, no, yeah, for sure.

00:40:29.580 --> 00:40:32.360
Yeah. I'm talking about the TV shows right now because the TV show came out.

00:40:32.360 --> 00:40:33.300
Oh, you're right. You're right.

00:40:33.380 --> 00:40:34.280
Okay. Copy, copy.

00:40:34.620 --> 00:40:37.080
Which is funny because that means that maybe they were thinking about that.

00:40:37.240 --> 00:40:42.000
Like, you know, we should get it on the NES because we're about to have this TV show.

00:40:43.020 --> 00:40:46.400
And we need people to become more familiar with it because let's be honest.

00:40:46.600 --> 00:40:51.660
Like, did everyone growing up have a Commodore 64?

00:40:52.600 --> 00:40:53.480
A television?

00:40:54.340 --> 00:40:56.240
Yeah, what is this? What is that?

00:40:56.300 --> 00:40:58.800
Back to the Future where he's like, yeah, we got one in every room.

00:40:58.940 --> 00:41:02.260
And they're like, what? I like the idea of people having NESs but not televisions.

00:41:03.080 --> 00:41:04.220
Right. What are they doing?

00:41:05.340 --> 00:41:09.980
No, I meant like those kinds of computers capable of playing those video games.

00:41:09.980 --> 00:41:15.700
Like, I don't know about you guys, but like we eventually got Windows 95 and it was amazing.

00:41:15.700 --> 00:41:18.060
But that was in 95, you know?

00:41:18.200 --> 00:41:22.260
Like, we didn't really have a computer capable of playing video games before that.

00:41:22.680 --> 00:41:23.720
I don't know, you know, like...

00:41:23.720 --> 00:41:25.120
You mean your house personally?

00:41:25.480 --> 00:41:26.280
My house personally.

00:41:26.280 --> 00:41:34.640
That's what I'm saying. Like, I don't know how many other people, though, have that experience of like, yeah, like we had a ZX Spectrum and my dad would always let me, you know, buy video games for me.

00:41:34.640 --> 00:41:41.360
And it's like, I don't think computer games were selling anywhere near the amount that NES games were selling.

00:41:41.620 --> 00:41:47.040
I don't know the numbers, but your vibes sound correct to me.

00:41:48.200 --> 00:41:49.940
Like, this is an expensive computer.

00:41:50.180 --> 00:41:53.100
I feel like they're not going to let, like, you know, the kid game on it.

00:41:54.020 --> 00:41:55.100
But again, just a vibe.

00:41:55.500 --> 00:41:56.760
We go by vibes here.

00:41:56.880 --> 00:41:58.060
Sorry. That's okay.

00:41:58.160 --> 00:41:59.160
Yeah, we're going by vibes.

00:41:59.160 --> 00:42:01.940
And speaking of vibes, the music in this game.

00:42:02.080 --> 00:42:03.040
What a vibe.

00:42:03.460 --> 00:42:04.400
Fantastic music.

00:42:04.700 --> 00:42:09.280
It's funny because I had in my notes, you know, I write my notes as I'm playing the game.

00:42:09.440 --> 00:42:17.880
I had that it's like a little annoying that the music is playing like in the house at full blast all the time as you're like exploring it.

00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:19.340
But I take that back.

00:42:20.300 --> 00:42:24.860
Like, I learned to love the, like, that each character has a song.

00:42:25.100 --> 00:42:31.460
And, you know, at first I guess I was thinking more like I wanted the mansion to have, like.

00:42:31.660 --> 00:42:32.900
A soundscape to it.

00:42:32.980 --> 00:42:34.300
Like, yeah, a soundscape to it.

00:42:34.400 --> 00:42:35.580
But, like, I don't know.

00:42:35.820 --> 00:42:40.820
These are pretty rocking tracks all throughout the game, including the main menu.

00:42:41.080 --> 00:42:45.060
The main menu is maybe one of my favorites so far on the NES.

00:42:45.060 --> 00:42:49.640
Yes. But, and I liked all of, I think all of these songs were great.

00:42:49.840 --> 00:42:53.860
I think they were some of the top tier NES songs we've had so far.

00:42:54.060 --> 00:42:58.380
But I did get annoyed with them after, you know, 10 minutes of each other.

00:42:58.780 --> 00:43:00.340
I turned them off.

00:43:00.460 --> 00:43:03.440
I was on as high on this, the soundtrack as you guys are.

00:43:03.780 --> 00:43:07.520
In fact, one of the first things I did was turn off each CD player.

00:43:08.320 --> 00:43:11.760
And. Yeah. I mean, I think it took me like 10 minutes to turn off the music.

00:43:11.760 --> 00:43:16.400
But. And then, like, I don't know if it was a bug or if this is just how it's, like, designed.

00:43:17.020 --> 00:43:20.540
But I went into another room and then the CD player turned on again.

00:43:20.840 --> 00:43:24.680
And then. But I hadn't turned the CD player back on.

00:43:24.860 --> 00:43:31.100
So I couldn't turn off the CD player until I then turned on the CD player again and turned it off again.

00:43:31.680 --> 00:43:34.020
And maybe it was just, like, going into the library.

00:43:34.020 --> 00:43:35.500
Is this where that bug is?

00:43:35.600 --> 00:43:37.220
Because I didn't really run into it again later.

00:43:38.120 --> 00:43:40.920
But that was like, is this game going to do this the whole time?

00:43:40.920 --> 00:43:47.080
Yeah. And the Famicom version of Maniac Mansion is.

00:43:48.440 --> 00:43:52.440
It's a bit different in the art style from the NES one.

00:43:52.520 --> 00:43:53.480
They're not the same.

00:43:53.740 --> 00:43:55.540
The characters are redrawn.

00:43:55.680 --> 00:43:58.140
Not to be, like, chibi or anything.

00:43:58.140 --> 00:44:01.000
But they don't look like how they look in the NES version.

00:44:01.460 --> 00:44:07.020
And that, like, the house in general is just, like, pretty damn empty.

00:44:07.020 --> 00:44:08.580
Like, it's a little strange.

00:44:08.740 --> 00:44:12.300
Like, you have to pull it up on your own to kind of see it, what I'm talking about.

00:44:12.440 --> 00:44:16.080
But, like, it is a different game in that regard.

00:44:16.300 --> 00:44:18.120
Like, even the UI is different.

00:44:18.320 --> 00:44:19.460
It's in boxes.

00:44:20.120 --> 00:44:27.200
Maybe it was just, like, they wanted to make it more familiar to the kind of adventure games that Japanese players were playing.

00:44:27.200 --> 00:44:35.360
But it's kind of weird that they're not just, like, you know, identical ports of each other with, like, maybe some difficulty changes or something like we're used to talking about.

00:44:35.580 --> 00:44:40.160
They're kind of two different games that ultimately play the same exact way.

00:44:40.160 --> 00:44:43.840
Just the way that they look is completely presented differently.

00:44:44.200 --> 00:44:50.860
If I'm looking at them correctly, it does look like it comes from a different system entirely.

00:44:51.620 --> 00:44:54.440
Like, it looks worse, right?

00:44:54.800 --> 00:44:56.480
Oh, yeah, way worse. It looks Game Boy-ish or something.

00:44:56.680 --> 00:44:59.360
Yeah, yeah, yeah. Yeah. Game Boy-ish is what I would say.

00:45:00.160 --> 00:45:03.960
I don't know if the Game Boy could do this, but I know what you're saying.

00:45:04.240 --> 00:45:05.620
But even just, like, you know what I'm talking about?

00:45:05.680 --> 00:45:08.540
Like, why is the house so empty?

00:45:08.800 --> 00:45:11.880
Like, I feel like there's barely anything to find inside of it.

00:45:12.060 --> 00:45:16.460
Yeah, I'm just looking at stills, so I can't really see the emptiness of the house.

00:45:16.600 --> 00:45:17.800
But the style looks very different.

00:45:17.800 --> 00:45:23.120
It's also, all the rooms are as wide as can be.

00:45:23.680 --> 00:45:26.880
You see everything in them from when you enter the room.

00:45:27.020 --> 00:45:33.820
Yeah, it looks less like we're trying to go for some sense of, like, perspective.

00:45:34.480 --> 00:45:38.000
And more, like, here's a video game room.

00:45:38.180 --> 00:45:42.500
I don't know. And, Sean, you talked about the sequel, Day of the Tentacle.

00:45:42.600 --> 00:45:54.040
Did you play it? No, that was, like, you know, that's one of the, like, highest rated, or not rated, but, like, the most respected point-and-click adventure games of all time.

00:45:54.240 --> 00:45:58.760
And I had no idea that it was related to this game at all until starting to play this.

00:45:58.840 --> 00:46:00.880
So I kind of want to play it now.

00:46:01.840 --> 00:46:03.120
What system is it on?

00:46:03.540 --> 00:46:04.880
PC. Oh, okay.

00:46:06.140 --> 00:46:09.920
Day of the Tentacle is, like, in huge green letters.

00:46:10.460 --> 00:46:15.280
And then, like, Maniac Mansion is just, like, tucked in there in the top left.

00:46:15.420 --> 00:46:16.460
Like, Maniac Mansion.

00:46:16.580 --> 00:46:17.480
Day of the Tentacle.

00:46:17.640 --> 00:46:19.320
You know, it's like, I can understand that.

00:46:20.000 --> 00:46:21.960
It looks like... I didn't know that was part of that.

00:46:22.280 --> 00:46:27.020
Yeah. I have not played the game, but it does look like that's the mansion in the background.

00:46:27.040 --> 00:46:34.940
And the Tentacle, obviously, is hopefully the same tentacles that we had fun with in the first Maniac Mansion.

00:46:35.260 --> 00:46:39.320
That's funny. Where was the day of the Tentacle TV show for three seasons?

00:46:40.700 --> 00:46:45.600
If you did get stuck as a kid, you weren't stuck forever.

00:46:45.600 --> 00:46:46.980
However, you had...

00:46:46.980 --> 00:46:49.700
Nintendo Power actually got a...

00:46:50.100 --> 00:46:52.580
Maniac Mansion got a cover issue of Nintendo Power.

00:46:52.740 --> 00:46:54.840
That's pretty big. To be on the cover?

00:46:55.060 --> 00:46:57.060
Yeah. Like, that means you're going to get...

00:46:57.060 --> 00:47:02.000
You know, you're going to basically get, like, half a game guide inside your issue and everything.

00:47:02.240 --> 00:47:05.100
So, which it did. So you could have found out about things that way.

00:47:05.420 --> 00:47:07.780
Or for just $7.95.

00:47:08.160 --> 00:47:09.560
And that includes shipping and handling.

00:47:09.660 --> 00:47:11.340
That was what I was going to talk about, yeah.

00:47:11.340 --> 00:47:16.620
$7.95, a hint book with puzzle solutions, a map of the mansion.

00:47:17.340 --> 00:47:21.280
That sounds like a great buy, considering the game was $50.

00:47:21.680 --> 00:47:24.380
What's another $10 to just, like, figure out what to do?

00:47:25.040 --> 00:47:26.420
Wow, the game was $50?

00:47:27.500 --> 00:47:29.040
All of them were, apparently.

00:47:29.600 --> 00:47:31.060
NES games? Wait, NES games?

00:47:31.800 --> 00:47:33.460
$49.95 is what I was seeing.

00:47:33.460 --> 00:47:36.860
Really? Wow. That's a lot higher than I thought they would have been.

00:47:37.160 --> 00:47:40.940
I think I've heard some Super Nintendo games were selling for, like, $80.

00:47:41.840 --> 00:47:44.980
Wow. I knew that the N64 got, like, really high.

00:47:45.100 --> 00:47:48.440
I just didn't realize that before that they were that high.

00:47:48.960 --> 00:47:53.000
Well, yeah. I feel like N64 games were also $50 for me growing up.

00:47:53.740 --> 00:47:55.440
Maybe there was an exception here or there.

00:47:55.960 --> 00:47:57.040
PS1 was $50.

00:47:57.540 --> 00:48:00.960
Yeah, and then I remember it was, like, PS2 where they became $60, right?

00:48:01.180 --> 00:48:03.340
Yeah. And it's kind of weird.

00:48:03.340 --> 00:48:05.980
No, I think it was PS1 and 2 that was $50.

